(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacodynamics-exam-2-antimicrobials-quiz-2"]={"title":"Antimicrobials Quiz — Set 2","category":"Pharmacodynamics","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Imipenem, meropenem, and ertapenem belong to which beta-lactam class?","choices":["Anti-staphylococcal penicillins","Monobactams","Cephalosporins","Carbapenems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are the carbapenems. — Slide 31"},{"q":"Which agent is bactericidal AND exhibits concentration-dependent killing?","choices":["Tetracycline","Erythromycin","Levofloxacin","Clarithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones like levofloxacin are bactericidal with concentration-dependent killing. — Slide 59"},{"q":"The natural penicillin spectrum notably includes which spirochete?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa strains","Treponema pallidum (syphilis)","Bacteroides fragilis anaerobes","Mycoplasma pneumoniae species"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin V covers Treponema pallidum (syphilis). — Slide 35"},{"q":"Photosensitivity plus deposition in developing teeth and bone are hallmark adverse effects of:","choices":["Fluoroquinolones","Vancomycin","Tetracyclines","Cephalosporins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines cause photosensitivity and deposit in teeth/bone (avoid in children/pregnancy). — Slide 51"},{"q":"Which class targets the bacterial cell wall but is NOT a beta-lactam?","choices":["Macrolide antibiotics","Tetracycline antibiotics","Fluoroquinolone antibiotics","Glycopeptides (vancomycin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is a glycopeptide that targets the cell wall without being a beta-lactam. — Slide 43"},{"q":"Clindamycin is described as:","choices":["Bacteriostatic","Bactericidal","Bacteriostatic only at high dose","Neither cidal nor static"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is bactericidal (macrolides are bacteriostatic, may be cidal at high doses). — Slide 52"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin generation has the MOST gram-positive and LEAST gram-negative coverage?","choices":["1st generation (cephalexin)","3rd generation (cefdinir)","4th generation (cefepime)","5th generation (ceftaroline)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. 1st generation (cephalexin) has the most gram-positive, least gram-negative coverage. — Slide 40"},{"q":"Which agent is preferred for Clostridium difficile when given orally, alongside oral vancomycin?","choices":["Metronidazole","Ciprofloxacin","Amoxicillin","Doxycycline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral metronidazole is indicated for C. difficile. — Slide 64"},{"q":"The mechanism of vancomycin is to:","choices":["Inactivate penicillin-binding proteins","Block cell wall cross-linking (glycopeptide)","Bind the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit","Inhibit bacterial DNA gyrase enzyme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin blocks cell wall cross-linking as a glycopeptide. — Slide 43"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone is specifically noted as NOT appropriate for UTI due to resistance concerns?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Levofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Moxifloxacin is specifically noted as NOT for UTI. — Slide 60"},{"q":"Amoxicillin differs from penicillin V primarily by having:","choices":["Reliable coverage against MRSA and MRSE strains","Expanded anaerobic coverage against Bacteroides fragilis","Meaningful activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa","More effective coverage against gram-negative bacilli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin shares penicillin V coverage but is more effective against gram-negative bacilli and widely used in respiratory infections. — Slide 36"},{"q":"Doxycycline is a recommended therapy for which STI-related or atypical scenario?","choices":["Pseudomonal pneumonia","Atypical pneumonia and STIs","Severe C. difficile colitis","Recurrent bacterial vaginosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is indicated for atypical pneumonia and STIs (as well as acne, RMSF, Lyme). — Slide 50"},{"q":"Fluoroquinolones are classified as:","choices":["Bactericidal, concentration-dependent, with prolonged PAE","Bacteriostatic, time-dependent with a short PAE","Bactericidal, time-dependent, with essentially no PAE","Bacteriostatic with a brief, moderate PAE only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones are bactericidal with concentration-dependent killing and a prolonged PAE. — Slide 59"},{"q":"Which beta-lactam class is represented by dicloxacillin, nafcillin, and oxacillin?","choices":["Penicillins with beta-lactamase inhibitors","Natural penicillins","Anti-pseudomonal penicillins","Anti-staphylococcal penicillins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are the anti-staphylococcal penicillins. — Slide 30"},{"q":"Erythromycin is clinically useful because its coverage is similar to penicillin, making it an option for patients who:","choices":["Currently have C. difficile","Have significant renal failure","Are currently pregnant","Have a penicillin allergy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Erythromycin's coverage resembles penicillin G/V and may be used in penicillin-allergic patients. — Slide 54"},{"q":"Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity are adverse effects associated with:","choices":["Amoxicillin","Vancomycin","Metronidazole","Clindamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is associated with nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity in addition to red man syndrome. — Slide 45"},{"q":"Which class provides broad-spectrum coverage of both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms and is bacteriostatic?","choices":["Tetracyclines","Natural penicillins","Glycopeptides","Monobactams"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines are broad-spectrum (gram-positive and gram-negative) and bacteriostatic. — Slide 49"},{"q":"Levofloxacin differs from ciprofloxacin by offering:","choices":["Complete loss of atypical pathogen coverage entirely","Expanded anaerobic coverage, including Bacteroides species","Improved gram-positive coverage plus Pseudomonas, useful in CAP","Reliable coverage against both MRSA and MRSE resistant strains"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin adds improved gram-positive activity and Pseudomonas coverage, making it useful for CAP. — Slide 61"},{"q":"Which agent's oral formulation is NOT systemically absorbed, which is why it works locally for gut infection?","choices":["Vancomycin","Levofloxacin","Doxycycline","Cephalexin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral vancomycin is not absorbed, acting locally in the gut for C. difficile colitis. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Metronidazole is indicated for which set of infections?","choices":["C. difficile, intra-abdominal, and vaginal infections (BV, trichomoniasis)","Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia, sinusitis, and acute bronchitis","MRSA skin, soft tissue, and bloodstream infections in hospitalized patients","Streptococcal pharyngitis and acute rheumatic fever prevention in children"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole treats C. difficile, intra-abdominal infections, and vaginal infections including BV and trichomoniasis. — Slide 64"},{"q":"The 5th-generation cephalosporin listed is:","choices":["Cefepime (Maxipime)","Ceftaroline (Teflaro)","Ceftriaxone (Rocephin)","Cefazolin (Ancef)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ceftaroline (Teflaro) is the listed 5th-generation cephalosporin. — Slide 40"},{"q":"Clindamycin's indications include all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Gram-negative aerobic UTI","Osteomyelitis","Intra-abdominal infection","Skin/soft tissue infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It has no gram-negative aerobic coverage, so a gram-negative aerobic UTI is not an indication. — Slide 55"},{"q":"Penicillins with beta-lactamase inhibitors (e.g., Augmentin, Unasyn) are indicated for:","choices":["Atypical, walking pneumonia caused specifically by Mycoplasma and Legionella species","Skin/soft tissue, diabetic foot ulcers, bites, and intra-abdominal infections","Vaginal trichomoniasis and bacterial vaginosis infections in women","Systemic MRSA bacteremia and infective endocarditis in high-risk patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These combinations are used for skin/soft tissue, diabetic foot ulcers, bites, and intra-abdominal infections. — Slide 42"},{"q":"Which class is bactericidal and uses time-dependent killing with no post-antibiotic effect?","choices":["Metronidazole","Fluoroquinolones","Tetracyclines","Cephalosporins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cephalosporins are bactericidal, time-dependent, with no PAE. — Slide 40"},{"q":"Cefepime is which generation of cephalosporin?","choices":["1st","2nd","3rd","4th"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cefepime is a 4th-generation cephalosporin. — Slide 31, 40"},{"q":"Metronidazole's mechanism is to:","choices":["Bind the 30S ribosomal subunit to block protein synthesis directly","Inactivate penicillin-binding proteins to block cell wall synthesis","Bind bacterial and parasitic proteins and DNA, inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Block peptidoglycan cross-linking as a glycopeptide antibiotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole binds bacterial/parasitic proteins and DNA to inhibit nucleic acid synthesis and also has anti-protozoal/anti-parasitic effect. — Slide 63"},{"q":"Which agent is used for MRSA and multidrug-resistant organisms among the tetracyclines?","choices":["Aztreonam","Cefazolin","Doxycycline","Penicillin V"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is listed for MRSA and multidrug-resistant organisms. — Slide 50"},{"q":"A metallic taste and nausea/vomiting with abdominal cramps are adverse effects noted for:","choices":["Cephalexin","Vancomycin","Metronidazole","Levofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole causes metallic taste, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal cramps. — Slide 65"},{"q":"Which class is used for community-acquired pneumonia due to atypicals like Legionella and Mycoplasma?","choices":["Anti-staphylococcal penicillins","Macrolides","Monobactams","Glycopeptides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Macrolides (e.g., clarithromycin, azithromycin) cover atypicals relevant to respiratory infections. — Slide 54"},{"q":"Azithromycin, compared with other macrolides, is:","choices":["Far more active against respiratory infections due to H. influenzae and M. catarrhalis","Considerably more active against staphylococcal skin infections specifically","Considered the preferred first-line drug for Pseudomonas infections","Uniquely bactericidal against essentially all gram-negative organisms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Azithromycin is far more active against respiratory infections due to H. influenzae and M. catarrhalis. — Slide 54"}]};
